  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "If \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are the roots of the equation \\(ax^2+bx+c=0,\\) the value of \\(\\dfrac1{r^2}+\\dfrac1{s^2}\\) is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(b^2-4ac\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{2a}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{c^2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{b^2-2ac}{c^2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "None of these"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1951-p47.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "By Vieta’s formulas, \\[ r+s=-\\frac ba,\\qquad rs=\\frac ca. \\] Therefore \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\frac1{r^2}+\\frac1{s^2} &=\\frac{(r+s)^2-2rs}{(rs)^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{b^2-2ac}{c^2}. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Write the expression over the common denominator \\(r^2s^2\\)",
        "Use \\(r+s=-\\frac{b}{a}\\) and \\(rs=\\frac{c}{a}\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
